 most record highs of any decade (56). Los Angeles' all-time high is 110\u00b0F, on September 26, 1963.","t_chg":"Los Angeles' days and nights both warmed. Nights warmed faster.","p_chg":"Across 81 complete years: Los Angeles' average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.48\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.36 to +0.59), about +3.8\u00b0F since 1945. Los Angeles' average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.13\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.00 to +0.27), about +1.1\u00b0F since 1945.","t_mon":"Lows rose in all 12 months. Highs rose in 2 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Los Angeles' yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.21 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 12.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 1983 (29.46 in); the driest, 1947 (3.12 in). Los Angeles almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 10 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}